From: Mohithash <shahjespuri@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 8 Sep 2026 11:19:37 +0000
Subject: WebView: leave the dangling raw_ptr detector off

Supporting-Dangling-Ptr-Detection-via-BackupRefPtr turns the detector
on by default for every target, and its runtime mode is to crash. In
the browser that is the user's choice through a setting. In WebView the
crash lands in the host application: on Android 17 the Vulkan draw
functor frees an object in one RenderThread callback and releases a
raw_ptr to it in the next whenever a page's renderer is replaced, so
any app that navigates dies within seconds. Upstream WebView carries
the same code and never enables the detector; disable it for WebView
alongside the other process-global allocator features.

License: GPL-2.0-or-later - https://spdx.org/licenses/GPL-2.0-or-later.html
---
 android_webview/browser/aw_field_trials.cc | 8 ++++++++
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+)

diff --git a/android_webview/browser/aw_field_trials.cc b/android_webview/browser/aw_field_trials.cc
index 274b0ec..97e18a3 100644
--- a/android_webview/browser/aw_field_trials.cc
+++ b/android_webview/browser/aw_field_trials.cc
@@ -261,6 +261,14 @@ void AwFieldTrials::RegisterFeatureOverrides(base::FeatureList* feature_list) {
   aw_feature_overrides.DisableFeature(
       base::features::kPartitionAllocMemoryTagging);
 
+  // Cromite compiles the dangling raw_ptr detector in and lets a browser
+  // setting decide at runtime, defaulting to crashing the process. WebView
+  // runs inside the host application's process, which has no way to opt out
+  // and no interest in a browser development aid, and upstream WebView never
+  // enables it. Keep it off like the other process-global allocator features.
+  aw_feature_overrides.DisableFeature(
+      base::features::kPartitionAllocDanglingPtr);
+
   // Disable Topics on WebView.
   aw_feature_overrides.DisableFeature(network::features::kBrowsingTopics);
 
